Chapter: EditorSave
  cites Works chapter ConsoleEditor
  cites Foreword chapter FileSystem

 BACKLOG 7.15: the console editor can save a file, and this is the test that
 says so. Until now editor-save called write-file, which stopped being a
 builtin and was never replaced, so the name resolved nowhere: ConsoleEditor
 died with CDX3002 and every chapter citing it died with it. The whole
 repository-protocol surface was dark behind one undefined name.

 The claim this pins is narrower and more useful than "it compiles". This
 program holds FileSystem.Read and FileSystem.Write and it holds NOTHING
 else -- no Device.Block. editor-save performs write-file as a bare effect
 op, so the compiler's default servicer (BACKLOG 1.14) picks it up, checks
 the granted direction bit, elevates, and drives fat16 on this program's
 behalf. So a save through the editor is a save through the FileSystem
 layer, and holding the layer never grants the disk.

 The grant is unscoped because editor-save is unscoped, and that is not an
 oversight in either place: an editor saves the path the user opened, so the
 function genuinely may touch anything. A scoped grant here is refused at
 compile time (CDX4002 -- the checker will not let a narrow grant service an
 unscoped performer), which is the scope machinery working. Scoping belongs
 to the application that mounts the editor, not to the editor.

 The read-back shares no code with the writer: read-text goes through the
 servicer to fat16-read-text, which walks the directory and follows the
 cluster chain. A save that reported success and wrote nothing would pass a
 test that only looked at editor-save's own answer, so this one looks at the
 bytes that came back off the volume instead.

 We say:

Section: Fixture

  seeded : EditorState
  seeded = EditorState {
    file-path = "SAVE.TXT",
    lines = ["hello editor"],
    line-count = 1,
    cursor-line = 0,
    cursor-col = 0,
    scroll-top = 0,
    visible-rows = 20,
    dirty = True,
    status = "modified"
  }

 The same buffer aimed at a subdirectory. This is the assertion that used to
 pin the OTHER half of BACKLOG 7.15: fat16-create-file refused any name with
 a path separator, so this save failed, and the point of pinning it was that
 it failed HONESTLY -- the editor reported the failure instead of reporting a
 save that never happened, which is what the old write-file builtin did.

 That gap is closed now, and this line is what said so: it was written as a
 tripwire, and it tripped. fat16-create-in-subdir resolves the parent with the
 read side's own walk and hands a byte address to the same create-at-slot the
 root uses, so the save lands. The disk fixture carries a real /SUB directory
 built host-side from the FAT16 spec (build/make-fat16-subdir.ps1).

 What this now proves is the whole stack in one line: the editor performs a
 bare write-file effect op, the compiler's default servicer picks it up,
 elevates, and drives fat16 into a subdirectory -- while this program holds
 FileSystem and never Device.Block.

  subdir : EditorState
  subdir = __record-set seeded "file-path" "SUB/NOTE.TXT"

 A path whose parent does not exist is still refused, and still refused
 honestly. That is the tripwire's replacement: the capability grew, so the
 assertion moved to the edge of what is still true rather than being deleted.

  orphan : EditorState
  orphan = __record-set seeded "file-path" "NOPE/X.TXT"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Read, FileSystem.Write] Nothing
  opening = act
    saved <- editor-save seeded
    print-line-uni ("status " & saved.status)
    print-line-uni ("dirty " & show (saved.dirty))

    back <- read-text "SAVE.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("readback " & back)

    sub <- editor-save subdir
    print-line-uni ("subdir-status " & sub.status)
    print-line-uni ("subdir-dirty " & show (sub.dirty))

    subback <- read-text "SUB/NOTE.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("subdir-readback " & subback)

    orph <- editor-save orphan
    print-line-uni ("orphan-status " & orph.status)
    print-line-uni ("orphan-dirty " & show (orph.dirty))
  end
